    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 1971 Nissan Skyline 2000GT-R Is Now an 833-Piece Mattel Brick Shop Set That Can Be Customized\nSubaru Recalls 2026 Crosstrek and Forester for Potential Control Arm Ball Joint Separation\n52 photos\nPhoto: Subaru\nThe supplier that builds front lower control arms for the Subaru Crosstrek and Forester is to blame for this recall. Several finished assemblies were parked before the last step of the assembly process, namely the final torquing of the castle nut that secures the ball joint. The parts shipped anyway, prompting the company to issue a do-not-drive advisory.\nSubaru's US subsidiary estimates that only eight such vehicles were shipped to dealers. The lower control arm in these vehicles locates the front wheel, letting it move up and down while keeping the wheel aligned with the rest of the suspension. Because of its shape, it's easy to understand that the ball joint acts as the pivot that lets the wheel travel over bumps and steer at the same time.\nWhen that joint separates, the wheel is no longer properly connected, increasing the risk of a crash. Separating ball joints cause a loss of control, and you certainly don't want to experience that while driving at high speed on the freeway. Subaru claims there is no warning before the failure, hence the do-not-drive instruction.\nSubaru of America explains in the recall filing published by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration that several left front lower control arm assemblies have been placed in the supplier's finished goods rack before the final castle nut torquing operation was completed. TOA of Indiana is the supplier in question.\nSuspect assemblies were shipped to Subaru of Indiana Automotive in Lafayette, where both the 2026 model year Crosstrek and Forester are produced for the US market. As of October 2026, the Lafayette-based plant also makes the Ascent people carrier. To prioritize high-volume SUVs and localized electrification, three models shifted production to Japan.\nPhoto: Subaru The Legacy went the way of the dodo in 2025 due to decreasing customer demand for sedans, whereas Outback production was consolidated at the Yajima plant in Japan. The Impreza hatchback also moved to Japan, and who could blame Scooby for it? After all, both the Crosstrek and the Forester are way more popular in the United States by sales volumes.\nUsing supplier process records and vehicle traceability records, Subaru of America singled out eight potentially affected Crosstreks and Foresters. All were assembled for the 2026 model year between August 4, 2026, and August 13, 2026. The company learned about the issue after a field report. Dated September 15, 2026, the report describes a ball joint separation.\nThe very next day, Subaru of America workers at the Lafayette plant started inspecting castle nuts on all completed vehicles at the facility. Subaru of America then confirmed the reported vehicle had no recorded torque inspection pass result, and that missing record prompted further investigation.\nDealers have already been instructed to inspect and replace all potentially affected crossovers. The left front lower control arm ball joint assembly will be replaced at no charge whatsoever, and the new assembly promises to have a properly tightened castle nut. Customer notifications are expected by December 16, 2026.\nBased on the same platform, the Crosstrek and Forester are both powered by four-pot boxers. Both can also be had as hybrids, and Subaru moves a lot of them. In year-to-date terms, the Crosstrek sold 138,249 units in the first nine months of 2026 as opposed to 156,476 examples for the larger Forester. The Outback is ranked third, clocking 104,759 deliveries through Q3 2026.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nSubaru Forester 2026 Subaru Forester Subaru Crosstrek 2026 Subaru Crosstrek Subaru recall\nDownload: 2026 Subaru Crosstrek and Forester castle nut recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT New Hot Wheels Red Line Club Exclusive 240Z Looks Like Double the Trouble",
